            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> The implied purpose of a \"URI\" is that _the URI_ has enough information to reproduce the content at any time. Like when you `:edit fugitive://...` or `:edit term://...` , the handlers for those URIs provide the relevant buffer contents. That is generally how URIs should work.\n\nIn context of Nvim **scratch** buffers URI is mostly a way to name a buffer that would not conflict with the path on disk. URI doesn't doesn't have to point at any resource per se.\n\nStoring in it info enough to 100% emulate the initial buffer creation sounds unrealistic. For example, if I run `:packupdate` + `:restart`, I do not expect and do not want it to once again to access the Internet to download the updates. The same thing with `:checkhealth` + `:restart` - I would not want it to run `:checkhealth` again.\n\nAlthough I think the current behavior is fine, the more perfect behavior would be that such special buffers are saved with more information (name+content+filetype) and restored with it. This would make `vim.pack` almost work as is (relying on the buffer id in the name is indeed the hurdle, but that is fixable with a bit more constraints). The same with buffers for `:checkhealth`, `:InspectTree` (maybe), `:EditQuery` (maybe).",
