            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Storing in it info enough to 100% emulate the initial buffer creation sounds unrealistic\n\nThat is not a requirement. Neither does `term://` do that. `nvim-pack://` would just rerun `:packupdate` (or maybe an \"offline\" version, idk). Doing anything else is not reasonable.\n\n> the more perfect behavior would be that such special buffers are saved with more information (name+content+filetype) and restored with it\n\nThen it would also be expected that sessions restore the buffer-local mappings, state, etc., which may depend on internal plugin state. That is a recipe for endless bugs. It would make more sense to serialize the entire Nvim state (which then defeats the purpose of a *restart*).\n\nA simple model is more useful, reliable, and easier for both users + plugin-authors to understand: \n\n- plugins decide how URIs are restored.\n- plugins should not attempt to save/restore \"state\". they should treat the URI the same regardless of sessions.",
