            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> `nvim-pack://` would just rerun `:packupdate` (or maybe an \"offline\" version, idk). Doing anything else is not reasonable.\n\nFor me personally (mostly as user) this exact behavior is not reasonable. For example, if restoring browser session would force refresh *all* of its opened tabs, this leads to a lot of unnecessary traffic+delay. The tab refresh should be on demand: be it after focusing on the tab or by explicit clicking the \"refresh\" button. With `vim.pack` this translates to either entering the buffer or user explicitly running `:packupdate` (which is what I'd expect here).\n\n> A simple model is more useful, reliable, and easier for both users + plugin-authors to understand:\n\nNow as a plugin author, this is not really easier to understand for me. How can Nvim know which plugin to ask to refresh the buffer and/or how can a plugin know *during startup* that it is supposed to do that for a particular buffer? Even with the case of `vim.pack`, would there be an hard-coded autocommand during startup outside of 'runtime/lua/vim/pack.lua', even though users might not even use `vim.pack`? And this doesn't even take into account lazy loaded plugins.",
